{"name":"Wool Skein","hex":"#d9cfba","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wool","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The hair of the sheep, llama and some other ruminants.","A cloth or yarn made from such hair."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wool"},{"term":"skein","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A quantity of thread, yarn, etc., wound on a reel then removed and loosely knotted into an oblong shape; a skein of cotton is formed by eighty turns of thread around a reel with a fifty-four inch diameter.","A thing resembling a skein (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1) of thread, yarn, etc.\n The membrane of a fish ovary.\n A group of wildfowl (for example, geese or swans) in flight."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To weave or wind (thread, yarn, etc.) into a skein (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1).","To intertwine or weave (something) with another thing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/skein"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wool","blocks":[{"text":"Wool is the textile fiber obtained from sheep and other mammals, especially goats, rabbits, and camelids. The term may also refer to inorganic materials, such as mineral wool and glass wool, that have some properties similar to animal wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg/330px-Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wool","wikidata":"Q42329"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg/500px-Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg/1280px-Sheep_eating_grass_edit02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm